#
# prima-materia — the searches of the run on the Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm, 2026-10-21
#
# In a file of their own for the mechanical reason recorded at
# examinations/searches-jain.ttl and in prima-materia#703.
#

pmp:GhayaSignsSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Every sign of success and of failure in the prologue and Book I of the Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm"@en, "Jedes Gelingens- und Misslingenszeichen im Prolog und im ersten Buch der Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm, prologue and Book I, chapters 1-7, read complete in Hashem Atallah's English (vol. I, pp. 1-61) and compared in Ritter's Arabic (archive.org picatrix-ghayat-al-hakim-al-majriti) at every passage entered; the Latin of Pingree's edition read complete for I.4 and at the passages of I.5 named in the nodes."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "Any sign by which the practitioner is told that a talisman or a work has taken effect or has not, any condition under which the text says the work fails, any account of why a work failed, and any claim that a recipe was tried."@en ;
    pm:searchTerms "In the Arabic: شرط and شروط (sharṭ, conditions), لا يتم (lā yatimm, is not completed), فسد and افسد (fasada, spoiled), محترق and احتراق (burned, burning), امتحن (imtaḥana, tested), مجرب (mujarrab, tried), عجبا (ʿajaban, a wonder); in Atallah's English: condition, without which, will not, spoil, damage, difficult, tested, miracles, see; in the Latin at I.4-5: probatum, expertus, destruit, gravius, sine dubio, tentandi."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:GhayaTilasmMayyit, pmc:GhayaTaliMuwajj, pmc:GhayaHimmaBeforeTamam, pmc:GhayaManzilaSadAlSuud, pmc:GhayaTilasmSaydAlHitanKhwarizmi, pmc:GhayaTilasmAlHasa ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-21" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-21" ;
    skos:note "Two counts, kept apart as SPEC §15 asks. Signs of success announced to the practitioner: none. The book promises outcomes, and twice says one will see wonders, at the talisman for a flourishing town and the physician's plate, but names nothing to watch for in the course of the work. Conditions of failure: four, the dead talisman made outside its ratios, the crooked or afflicted ascendant, the Art attempted in the twenty-fourth mansion, and the lack of the maker's gathered resolve. Two recipes carry a claim of trial, both without a case: one ascribed to al-Khwārizmī, one in the compiler's own two words."@en .

pmp:GhayaTermSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Whether Book I of the Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm names a term after which absence counts against a work"@en, "Ob das erste Buch der Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm eine Frist nennt, nach der das Ausbleiben gegen ein Werk zählt"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The same prologue and seven chapters in the same three witnesses."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "A number of days, months or years, or a number of attempts, within which a talisman is to take effect, after which its absence is a verdict, or for which its effect is said to last."@en ;
    pm:searchTerms "In the Arabic: يوم and ايام (yawm, days), شهر (shahr, month), سنة (sana, year), ساعة (sāʿa, hour, in the sense of at once), ميل (mīl, mile), ما دام (mā dāma, as long as); in Atallah's English: day, days, month, year, immediately, as long as, forever; in the Latin at the same places: dies, miliaria, statim."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundNothing ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-21" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-21" ;
    skos:note "No term after which absence counts. What comes nearest is of three kinds, none of them a term. Promises of immediacy: the enemy is destroyed within the hour, the expelled man leaves his town the same day. A duration bound to the object: the man stays in his town as long as the talisman is buried. And one reading that would be a term, which is not the text: at the scorpion talisman Ritter's text and the Latin say the scorpions keep away for forty-five miles, and Atallah's English, following the apparatus, says forty-five days over one mile. The days are the only number of time in the translation and they belong to a variant."@en .

pmp:GhayaLetterSearch a pm:Searching ;
    rdfs:label "Whether Book I of the Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm carries the magic of letters and names the registration names"@en, "Ob das erste Buch der Ghāyat al-Ḥakīm die Buchstaben- und Namensmagie trägt, die die Registrierung nennt"@de ;
    pm:searchedCorpus "The same prologue and seven chapters in the same three witnesses."@en ;
    pm:searchedFor "Any operation with letters, letter squares, the divine names or the Greatest Name, since the registration defines this tradition by them and names this work in its principal corpus."@en ;
    pm:searchTerms "In the Arabic: حروف and حرف (ḥurūf, letters), الاسماء الالهية (al-asmāʾ al-ilāhiyya, the divine names), الاسم الاعظم (al-ism al-aʿẓam, the Greatest Name), وفق (wafq, magic square), جدول (jadwal, table); in Atallah's English: letter, alphabet, name, names of God, square."@en ;
    pm:searchOutcome pm:searchFoundPlace ;
    pm:searchYieldedNode pmc:GhayaIstinzalYaqtul ;
    pm:searchedBy "prima-materia, 2026-10-21" ;
    dcterms:date "2026-10-21" ;
    skos:note "Three places and no operation. The twenty-eight letters are matched to the twenty-eight mansions in I.6, as one correspondence of man the small world among many. The divine names occur in the warning ascribed to Aristotle that some of them bring the spirits down fast enough to kill, and the Greatest Name in the remark on the Sufis beside it. No letter square, no table of letters and no recipe working by a name. The numbers of the loving-numbers talisman are counts of units, not letters. Book I of this work is astral talisman magic, and the registration's definition fits the Šams al-maʿārif, which is unopened, and not what was read."@en .
